  "pendingTickets": [
    {
      "key": "parsing-correction-feedback-loop",
      "title": "Wire parsing correction feedback loop — RecipeDiffCalculator → Firestore",
      "labels": [
        "idea",
        "parsing"
      ],
      "priority": 4,
      "description": "## Opportunity\n`RecipeDiffCalculator` computes exactly what the parser got wrong vs. user corrections. `ParsingCorrection.create()` is called but never written to Firestore. Free training data for improving the parser is being silently discarded.\n\n## Current State\nThe infrastructure is 90% built: `RecipeDiffCalculator` in `lib/services/parsing/feedback/recipe_diff_calculator.dart` produces `ParsingCorrection` objects, but they're never persisted.\n\n## Proposed Improvement\nCreate `ParsingFeedbackService` that writes `ParsingCorrection` to `parsing_corrections` Firestore collection when user saves an edited imported recipe. Security rules needed for new collection.\n\n## Effort vs Impact\nLow effort (infrastructure exists). Medium-term impact: enables data-driven parser improvement over time.\n\n**Files:** `lib/services/parsing/feedback/recipe_diff_calculator.dart`, new `parsing_feedback_service.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "comment-engagement-preview-edit-reactions",
      "title": "Improve comment engagement: inline preview, edit capability, reaction discoverability",
      "labels": [
        "idea",
        "social",
        "recipe"
      ],
      "priority": 3,
      "description": "## Opportunity\nThree comment UX gaps: (a) collapsed header shows only \"Kommentarer\" + count — no preview of latest comment; (b) users can delete but not edit own comments; (c) long-press emoji reaction picker has zero visual hint — completely undiscoverable.\n\n## Current State\nComments section collapses to a count badge. No edit capability. Reaction discovery relies entirely on users guessing long-press behavior.\n\n## Proposed Improvement\n(a) Show first/latest comment author + truncated text when collapsed (Instagram-style).\n(b) Add `onEdit` callback parallel to `onDelete` with inline text field.\n(c) Add subtle \"Håll inne för reaktion\" hint or tooltip on first interaction.\n\n## Effort vs Impact\nMedium effort across 2 widget files. High engagement payoff — comment interactions drive social stickiness.\n\n**Files:** `lib/views/recipe_detail/recipe_detail_comments.dart`, `lib/widgets/recipe/comment_item_widgets.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "warm-dark-color-scheme",
      "title": "Handcraft warm dark color scheme preserving brand identity",
      "labels": [
        "idea",
        "recipe"
      ],
      "priority": 3,
      "description": "## Opportunity\nCurrent dark mode uses `ColorScheme.fromSeed()` which generates cool M3 dark surfaces (#1C1B1F). The entire warm cream personality disappears. The brand is warm food colors — dark mode should be warm dark brown (#1A1611), not cold gray.\n\n## Current State\nDark mode auto-generates cold gray surfaces. `ButleryColors.dark` already has `copyWith` and `lerp` for smooth transitions.\n\n## Proposed Improvement\nReplace auto-generated dark surfaces with handcrafted warm dark palette. Also: delete legacy `AppColors.secondaryPurple` (off-brand, only used in shader warm-up).\n\n## Effort vs Impact\nMedium effort — theme files only. High brand payoff: dark mode finally feels like Butlery.\n\n**Files:** `lib/theme/app_theme.dart`, `lib/theme/app_colors.dart`, `lib/theme/butlery_colors.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "seasonal-accent-color-variations",
      "title": "Add seasonal accent color variations",
      "labels": [
        "idea",
        "recipe"
      ],
      "priority": 4,
      "description": "## Opportunity\nThe rust + forest green palette is static year-round. `ButleryColors` has `copyWith` — the mechanism to do month-based accent shifts exists. Warm amber tones in autumn, cooler blue-tinted cream in winter would give the app a living feel.\n\n## Current State\nStatic color palette year-round.\n\n## Proposed Improvement\n`SeasonalAccentService` reads current month, returns a `ButleryColors` override. Subtle — not a full re-theme, just accent warmth shifts. Could use `AnimatedTheme` for smooth transitions on month boundaries.\n\n## Effort vs Impact\nMedium effort (new service + theme integration). Delightful — gives the app personality that changes with the seasons.\n\n**Files:** New `lib/services/theme/seasonal_accent_service.dart`, `lib/theme/butlery_colors.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "data-export-broken-web",
      "title": "Fix data export broken on web platform",
      "labels": [
        "Bug",
        "account"
      ],
      "priority": 2,
      "description": "## Finding\n`DataExportView._handleDownload()` uses `getApplicationDocumentsDirectory()` which returns an in-memory VFS path on web — the file is written somewhere the user can never find. No `kIsWeb` guard. The share flow works, but the explicit \"Save file\" button is silently broken on web.\n\n## Why It Matters\nUsers on web cannot export their data — a GDPR requirement and basic user expectation.\n\n## Suggested Fix\nAdd `kIsWeb` check — on web, use `dart:html` `AnchorElement` download or redirect to share flow. Pattern already used in `lib/main.dart:126`.\n\n**Files:** `lib/views/account/data_export_view.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "in-app-notification-inbox",
      "title": "Add in-app notification inbox view",
      "labels": [
        "idea",
        "social"
      ],
      "priority": 3,
      "description": "## Opportunity\nNotifications only appear in the OS tray. Dismissed = lost forever. But `NotificationAnalyticsManager` stores notification history via `recordNotification`, `markNotificationDelivered`, `markNotificationOpened`. The data exists in Firestore — no view surfaces it.\n\n## Current State\nNo in-app notification history. OS tray is the only channel.\n\n## Proposed Improvement\nSimple `NotificationsView` backed by `_repository.getNotificationHistory(userId)` — list of recent notifications with tap-to-navigate. Could live under the social tab or as a bell icon in the app bar.\n\n## Effort vs Impact\nMedium effort (new view + viewmodel). High engagement — users never miss social actions.\n\n**Files:** New `lib/views/notifications/notifications_view.dart`, `lib/viewmodels/notifications_viewmodel.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "search-history-algolia-highlights",
      "title": "Add search history persistence and Algolia highlight rendering",
      "labels": [
        "idea",
        "recipe"
      ],
      "priority": 3,
      "description": "## Opportunity\n(a) No search history stored — users start fresh every session. `RecipeQueryViewModel` tracks `_lastSearchQuery` only as a cache key. (b) `RecipeSearchHit` carries `highlightedTitle`/`highlightedDescription` from Algolia but they're never rendered — matches aren't visually scannable.\n\n## Current State\nSearch is ephemeral with no history. Algolia highlight data is fetched but discarded at render time.\n\n## Proposed Improvement\n(a) Persist `List<String> recentSearches` (max 10) in SharedPreferences, show in suggestion overlay when search focused but empty.\n(b) Render Algolia `<em>` tags as bold spans in search result cards.\n\n## Effort vs Impact\nLow-medium effort. High UX payoff — search feels smarter and faster.\n\n**Files:** `lib/viewmodels/recipe_query_viewmodel.dart`, `lib/widgets/recipe/recipe_card.dart`, `lib/repositories/algolia/algolia_search_repository.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "pantry-inventory-cook-with-what-i-have",
      "title": "Add pantry inventory with \"Laga med vad jag har\" filter",
      "labels": [
        "idea",
        "recipe",
        "shopping"
      ],
      "priority": 3,
      "description": "## Opportunity\nTop recipe apps (Samsung Food, KitchenPal, Cooklist) have pantry tracking. The killer feature is \"cook with what I have\" — filtering recipes by on-hand ingredients. Butlery has deep ingredient taxonomy (`IngredientLookupService`, `ingredient_data.dart`) but zero pantry tracking.\n\n## Current State\nNo pantry concept exists. Ingredient taxonomy is rich but unused for inventory.\n\n## Proposed Improvement\n`PantryItem` model (ingredient, quantity, optional expiry) + `PantryService` + \"Laga med vad jag har\" filter on recipe list. Connects naturally into existing ingredient taxonomy. Also a monetization hook.\n\n## Effort vs Impact\nLarge effort (new models, service, views, filter integration). Very high impact — a differentiating feature that leverages existing taxonomy.\n\n**Files:** New `lib/models/pantry/pantry_item.dart`, `lib/services/pantry/pantry_service.dart`, `lib/views/pantry/pantry_view.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "tag-collection-insights",
      "title": "Add tag-based collection insights for users",
      "labels": [
        "idea",
        "tagging",
        "analytics"
      ],
      "priority": 4,
      "description": "## Opportunity\nButlery's auto-tagging engine produces rich dietary/allergen/cuisine data per recipe — unique in the market. But users never see aggregate insights. \"Your collection is 43% vegetarian, 12% vegan, top cuisine: Italian\" would showcase the tagging engine's value and help users understand their cooking patterns.\n\n## Current State\nRich per-recipe tag data exists. No aggregation or insights view.\n\n## Proposed Improvement\nCollection insights widget on profile or recipe list — pie chart or simple stat cards powered by existing tag data. No new computation needed — just aggregate what auto-tags already produce.\n\n## Effort vs Impact\nMedium effort (new widget + aggregation logic). High delight — users discover something about themselves.\n\n**Files:** New `lib/widgets/recipe/collection_insights_widget.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "cooking-challenges-time-bounded-tags",
      "title": "Add cooking challenges via time-bounded personal tag rules",
      "labels": [
        "idea",
        "tagging",
        "social"
      ],
      "priority": 4,
      "description": "## Opportunity\nCookpad's engagement secret is challenges (\"cook something new every week\"). Butlery's personal tag + rule system + `retagging_scheduler.dart` already handles scheduled re-evaluation. Time-bounded challenges could be built entirely on existing infrastructure.\n\n## Current State\nPersonal tag rules support conditions but not date ranges. Retagging scheduler exists.\n\n## Proposed Improvement\nAdd `dateRange` condition to personal tag rules — a \"Mars-utmaningen\" tag auto-applied to newly cooked recipes during March. Leverage existing rule engine + scheduler. Social sharing of challenge completion optional.\n\n## Effort vs Impact\nMedium effort (extend rule model + scheduler). High engagement — gamification drives retention.\n\n**Files:** `lib/models/personal_tag/personal_tag_rule.dart`, `lib/services/tagging/retagging_scheduler.dart`"
    },
    {
      "key": "tag-decision-audit-trail",
      "title": "TagDecision audit trail for tag generation",
      "labels": [
        "Enhancement",
